Charlie <choochoocharlie@spamherelots.com> wrote:

> Is there any particular game advantage to one or the other, perhhaps
> varying according to role?

None, really. Well, none that are significant.

First, most differences are cosmetic. For example, nymphs "seduce" men
but "charm" women; however, the result is exactly the same.

The real differences are:
- Valks can only be female. Hardly an advantage of the gender, since you
  choose your role first anyway.
- Female characters poly into female monsters, who may lay eggs if the
  poly'd species can. Male characters poly into male monsters, who don't
  lay eggs (there not being a "cock" species in the game; one wonders
  where the cockatrice eggs come from).
- If an egg hatches in your pack, they're tame 50% of the time,
  randomly, if you're a man; if you're a woman, they're tame only if you
  laid them (but then all the time).
- Men can, ahem, concupiscate (only) with succubi; women (only) with
  incubi. The results of the proceedings are exactly the same for
  either, though, except for:
- A succubus will steal your rings of adornment if you are a man, making
  the result of the following fornication less fevourable to you. If
  you're a woman, an incubus may put a ring of adornment on you,
  possibly replacing a ring you were wearing, which can have unpleasant
  consequences (but does make the following rumpty-tumpty more positive
  for you).

So. As a woman you're guaranteed pets from your eggs, but you have to
self-poly to get them; as a man you need a bit of luck, but don't need
to break self-poly conduct. As a man rings of adornment are useless to
you, at least as far as succubi are concerned; as a woman, they can be
downright dangerous if you were wearing, e.g., a ring of levitation and
meet one of the boys at Medusa's, but may also help your chances.

IMO, they're much of a muchness, to any practical extent at least.

